  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 948 AM MST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nTODAY & FRIDAY'S PRECIPITATION CHANCES:\n\nAn upstream cyclone continues moving onshore into the Pacific \nNorthwest today, with its associated moisture streaming into the \nIntermountain West this afternoon on strong southerly flow aloft. \nThis moisture plume will reach our western CWA by late afternoon as \nthe nose of the jet pushes into the region, aligning along an axis \nfrom southeast Utah to the northern mountains by Thursday nightfall. \nCurrent high-resolution guidance indicates weak instability \naccompanying this system, which will likely result in streaky, \nbanded precipitation. Due to this convective nature, confidence \nremains low regarding widespread or prolonged impacts.  \n\nThat said, there remains a roughly 70% probability of 4 to 6 inches \nof snow across the higher elevations of the Elk, West Elk, Flat \nTops, and Park ranges. The eastern Uintas will see a slightly higher \nceiling, with a ~70% chance of 4 to 8 inches due to a longer \nduration of snowfall compared to surrounding areas. Across the open \ntravel corridors, expected accumulations are lower, and hazardous \ntravel impacts remain uncertain. Meanwhile, persistent moist \nsoutherly flow across the southern mountains will keep light \nflurries lingering over the highest peaks, though the deepest \nmoisture will exit the region before the main axis of instability \nshifts east Friday afternoon.\n\nWind will be an additional concern during this forecast period. As \nthe jet noses into the region, gusty winds will develop today and \npersist into Friday, with gusts reaching up to 45 mph in spots, \nparticularly across higher elevations. Combined with falling or \nfresh snow, these gusts could produce localized areas of blowing \nsnow and reduced visibility.  \n\nSATURDAY + BEYOND: \n\nBy Saturday, a trailing lobe of energy will rotate through the Great \nBasin toward the Four Corners. Although deep moisture will be \nlargely scoured out by this point, dynamic forcing and increasing \nsnow ratios could still trigger a brief burst of heavier snowfall \nrates over the southern mountains. Because this wave will be \nrelatively fast-moving, accumulations over the southern mountains \nshould generally remain around 3 to 6 inches. Temperatures will also \ntrend colder behind this system this weekend, bringing single-digit \nlows to higher elevations.  \n\nUnsettled weather will return to the region during Thanksgiving \nweek, though specific timing and track details remain uncertain. \nEven so, a series of disturbances propagating through the area will \nlikely bring minor travel impacts to the mountains. Continue to \nmonitor the latest forecasts as model consensus improves on the \ntiming of these waves. Daily highs and lows will also trend roughly \n5 degrees below seasonal normals.",
